1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,383
When you ask a large language model a question,

2
00:00:02,383 --> 00:00:04,309
the answer appears one word at a time,

3
00:00:04,309 --> 00:00:05,982
almost like someone is typing it.

4
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,521
Each of those little pieces of text,

5
00:00:08,521 --> 00:00:12,131
called tokens, is the output of an enormous computation:

6
00:00:12,131 --> 00:00:16,257
billions of multiplications, carried out on a chip called a GPU.

7
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:18,728
In this video, let's open up that chip:

8
00:00:18,728 --> 00:00:20,727
from how the hardware is organized,

9
00:00:20,727 --> 00:00:24,154
all the way up to the tricks that make modern chatbots fast.

10
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,537
And along the way we'll run into a surprising fact.

11
00:00:27,537 --> 00:00:30,613
For most of the time a GPU spends generating text,

12
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:32,520
the bottleneck is not the math.

13
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,796
The arithmetic units sit mostly idle.

14
00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:37,626
The real limit is something much more mundane:

15
00:00:37,626 --> 00:00:40,518
how quickly you can move numbers out of memory.

16
00:00:40,767 --> 00:00:44,007
To see why, let's start with a more basic question.

17
00:00:44,007 --> 00:00:47,820
What even makes a GPU different from the CPU in your laptop?

18
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:52,934
A CPU is a brilliant generalist:

19
00:00:52,934 --> 00:00:54,860
a handful of very powerful cores,

20
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:59,528
each surrounded by large caches and clever control logic that predicts branches,

21
00:00:59,528 --> 00:01:04,371
reorders instructions, and does everything it can to finish a single stream of work

22
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:05,539
as fast as possible.

23
00:01:05,767 --> 00:01:07,395
A GPU makes the opposite bet.

24
00:01:07,395 --> 00:01:10,989
It strips each core down to little more than an arithmetic unit,

25
00:01:10,989 --> 00:01:13,010
and then packs in thousands of them.

26
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:15,368
Any one of these cores is slow and simple,

27
00:01:15,368 --> 00:01:17,783
but together they have enormous throughput.

28
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,587
Think of it as a few brilliant professors versus a stadium full of students

29
00:01:22,587 --> 00:01:24,911
who each know how to multiply and add.

30
00:01:24,911 --> 00:01:28,336
For a hard, sequential problem, you want the professors.

31
00:01:28,336 --> 00:01:31,272
For millions of independent little calculations,

32
00:01:31,272 --> 00:01:33,229
the stadium wins by a landslide.

33
00:01:33,467 --> 00:01:37,702
And it just so happens that the core operation of neural networks is exactly

34
00:01:37,702 --> 00:01:40,154
that kind of problem: matrix multiplication.

35
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,407
Each entry of the output is a dot product between one row of the

36
00:01:44,407 --> 00:01:47,036
first matrix and one column of the second.

37
00:01:47,036 --> 00:01:49,603
Crucially, no entry depends on any other.

38
00:01:49,603 --> 00:01:52,545
So you can hand each one to a different worker,

39
00:01:52,545 --> 00:01:54,924
and compute them all at the same time.

40
00:01:55,167 --> 00:01:56,935
This is the whole reason GPUs,

41
00:01:56,935 --> 00:01:59,999
originally built to color in pixels for video games,

42
00:01:59,999 --> 00:02:02,120
ended up powering the AI revolution.

43
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:06,894
Shading millions of pixels and multiplying giant matrices turn out to be the same

44
00:02:06,894 --> 00:02:07,601
kind of job.

45
00:02:10,833 --> 00:02:13,352
Let's take a concrete example: NVIDIA's H100,

46
00:02:13,352 --> 00:02:17,103
one of the workhorse chips for running large language models today.

47
00:02:17,333 --> 00:02:19,787
Most of the chip is this repeating tile,

48
00:02:19,787 --> 00:02:22,364
called a Streaming Multiprocessor, or S M.

49
00:02:22,364 --> 00:02:23,898
The H100 has 132 of them.

50
00:02:23,898 --> 00:02:26,781
Through the middle runs a 50 megabyte L2 cache,

51
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:30,646
shared by all of them. And right next to the chip sit stacks of

52
00:02:30,646 --> 00:02:32,425
high bandwidth memory, H B M,

53
00:02:32,425 --> 00:02:36,168
holding 80 gigabytes. This is where the model's weights live.

54
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:37,985
Let's zoom in on one SM.

55
00:02:37,985 --> 00:02:41,354
It has four quadrants, each with its own scheduler,

56
00:02:41,354 --> 00:02:45,119
a big register file, a few dozen simple arithmetic units,

57
00:02:45,119 --> 00:02:49,148
the so-called CUDA cores, and one much more specialized unit:

58
00:02:49,148 --> 00:02:52,649
a Tensor Core. Shared across the whole SM is a small,

59
00:02:52,649 --> 00:02:56,215
fast scratchpad of memory, a couple hundred kilobytes,

60
00:02:56,215 --> 00:02:58,395
that programmers manage directly.

61
00:02:58,633 --> 00:03:02,561
The work given to a GPU is organized in a similar hierarchy.

62
00:03:02,561 --> 00:03:04,525
The smallest unit is a thread:

63
00:03:04,525 --> 00:03:09,172
one worker. Threads are bundled into groups of thirty two called warps,

64
00:03:09,172 --> 00:03:12,838
which all execute the same instruction at the same time,

65
00:03:12,838 --> 00:03:16,700
each on its own data. Warps are grouped into thread blocks,

66
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:20,759
each running on a single SM and sharing its scratchpad memory.

67
00:03:20,759 --> 00:03:22,592
And the whole job, the grid,

68
00:03:22,592 --> 00:03:26,454
can contain thousands of blocks, spread across all the SMs.

69
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:30,133
The Tensor Cores do almost all the heavy lifting for A I.

70
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:33,325
Instead of multiplying one pair of numbers at a time,

71
00:03:33,325 --> 00:03:35,614
a Tensor Core takes in small matrices,

72
00:03:35,614 --> 00:03:38,927
multiplies them, and adds the result to an accumulator,

73
00:03:38,927 --> 00:03:40,493
all as a single operation.

74
00:03:40,733 --> 00:03:46,071
Add it all up, and an H100 can perform roughly a thousand trillion floating

75
00:03:46,071 --> 00:03:50,057
point operations every second, at sixteen bit precision.

76
00:03:50,057 --> 00:03:52,619
That's a petaflop, on a single chip.

77
00:03:52,867 --> 00:03:56,539
But here's the catch. Those numbers have to come from somewhere.

78
00:03:56,539 --> 00:04:00,096
And the different kinds of memory on the GPU form a hierarchy,

79
00:04:00,096 --> 00:04:02,793
with a brutal trade-off between size and speed.

80
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:05,046
At the top are the registers,

81
00:04:05,046 --> 00:04:09,348
tiny but instantaneous. Then the shared memory inside each SM.

82
00:04:09,348 --> 00:04:11,916
Then the L2 cache. And at the bottom,

83
00:04:11,916 --> 00:04:13,720
the 80 gigabytes of H B M,

84
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:18,994
which can deliver data at about three point three five terabytes per second.

85
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:21,979
That sounds fast, but compare it to the compute.

86
00:04:21,979 --> 00:04:26,898
A thousand teraflops divided by three point three five terabytes per second means that

87
00:04:26,898 --> 00:04:28,899
for every byte you pull from H B M,

88
00:04:28,899 --> 00:04:31,645
the GPU could do about three hundred operations.

89
00:04:31,645 --> 00:04:33,475
Do less work than that per byte,

90
00:04:33,475 --> 00:04:35,763
and the arithmetic units just sit there,

91
00:04:35,763 --> 00:04:36,735
waiting for data.

92
00:04:39,967 --> 00:04:42,597
This ratio, operations performed per byte moved,

93
00:04:42,597 --> 00:04:47,475
is called arithmetic intensity, and it's the single most useful number for thinking about

94
00:04:47,475 --> 00:04:48,352
GPU performance.

95
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:53,610
Take matrix multiplication again. If every output entry naively went back to main memory

96
00:04:53,610 --> 00:04:55,887
to fetch its own row and its own column,

97
00:04:55,887 --> 00:04:58,619
the same numbers would be fetched over and over,

98
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:00,669
and the intensity would be terrible.

99
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:04,647
Instead, well-written GPU code uses a technique called tiling.

100
00:05:04,647 --> 00:05:08,939
A thread block loads one square tile of each input matrix into the fast

101
00:05:08,939 --> 00:05:13,835
shared memory, just once. Then every thread in the block reuses those values many

102
00:05:13,835 --> 00:05:17,885
times, accumulating partial results for a whole tile of the output.

103
00:05:17,885 --> 00:05:20,544
Then it slides on to the next pair of tiles.

104
00:05:20,767 --> 00:05:24,593
The bigger the tile, the more reuse you get out of every byte.

105
00:05:24,593 --> 00:05:25,766
For an n by n tile,

106
00:05:25,766 --> 00:05:28,420
you move on the order of n squared numbers,

107
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:31,012
but do on the order of n cubed operations.

108
00:05:31,012 --> 00:05:34,037
So the intensity grows with the size of the tile.

109
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:38,373
A nice way to picture all of this is with what's called a roofline

110
00:05:38,373 --> 00:05:41,545
plot. On the horizontal axis, arithmetic intensity.

111
00:05:41,545 --> 00:05:45,029
On the vertical axis, the actual throughput you achieve.

112
00:05:45,267 --> 00:05:48,575
At low intensity you're limited by memory bandwidth.

113
00:05:48,575 --> 00:05:51,438
Your speed is just bandwidth times intensity:

114
00:05:51,438 --> 00:05:56,018
this slanted line. At high intensity you hit the ceiling of how fast the

115
00:05:56,018 --> 00:05:58,881
chip can compute. The corner where they meet,

116
00:05:58,881 --> 00:06:02,316
around three hundred operations per byte for the H100,

117
00:06:02,316 --> 00:06:03,970
is called the ridge point.

118
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:06,467
Everything to the left is memory-bound.

119
00:06:06,467 --> 00:06:08,849
Everything to the right is compute-bound.

120
00:06:08,849 --> 00:06:13,208
Keep this picture in mind, because the story of LLM inference is really the

121
00:06:13,208 --> 00:06:16,869
story of where on this plot each part of the computation lands.

122
00:06:20,096 --> 00:06:23,348
Quick refresher on what a language model actually computes.

123
00:06:23,348 --> 00:06:25,388
If you've seen how transformers work,

124
00:06:25,388 --> 00:06:26,601
this will be familiar.

125
00:06:26,829 --> 00:06:28,663
The text is broken into tokens,

126
00:06:28,663 --> 00:06:31,917
and each token is turned into a long vector of numbers.

127
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:34,757
These vectors flow through a sequence of layers.

128
00:06:34,757 --> 00:06:39,963
In each layer, an attention block lets the vectors exchange information with each other,

129
00:06:39,963 --> 00:06:43,809
and a multilayer perceptron block updates each vector on its own.

130
00:06:44,029 --> 00:06:48,930
At the end, the last vector is turned into a probability distribution over every

131
00:06:48,930 --> 00:06:51,136
possible next token. One is sampled,

132
00:06:51,136 --> 00:06:54,322
appended to the text, and the whole process repeats.

133
00:06:54,563 --> 00:06:58,453
Notice that almost every step here is a matrix multiplication:

134
00:06:58,453 --> 00:07:00,460
a big matrix of learned weights,

135
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:04,162
multiplied by the token vectors. The attention projections,

136
00:07:04,162 --> 00:07:06,546
the MLP layers, the final unembedding.

137
00:07:06,546 --> 00:07:08,617
All weights. All matrix products.

138
00:07:08,863 --> 00:07:11,057
And there are a lot of weights.

139
00:07:11,057 --> 00:07:14,809
An open model like Llama 3 with 8 billion parameters,

140
00:07:14,809 --> 00:07:17,712
at two bytes each, takes up 16 gigabytes,

141
00:07:17,712 --> 00:07:20,190
and frontier models are far larger.

142
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:25,712
For our running example, let's put that 8 billion parameter model on one H100.

143
00:07:28,929 --> 00:07:31,881
Generating a response actually happens in two very different phases.

144
00:07:32,929 --> 00:07:35,651
First comes prefill: reading your prompt,

145
00:07:35,651 --> 00:07:39,435
say, a thousand tokens. Since they're all known up front,

146
00:07:39,435 --> 00:07:41,692
they can be processed in parallel.

147
00:07:41,692 --> 00:07:47,401
Each weight matrix multiplies a big matrix holding all thousand token vectors at once.

148
00:07:47,629 --> 00:07:52,388
That means every weight we load from memory gets reused a thousand times.

149
00:07:52,388 --> 00:07:58,385
The arithmetic intensity is high, and prefill lands comfortably on the compute-bound side of

150
00:07:58,385 --> 00:08:01,840
the roofline. The GPU is doing what it was built for.

151
00:08:02,063 --> 00:08:04,651
Then comes decode, generating the answer.

152
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:08,944
And here's the problem. Each new token depends on the one before it,

153
00:08:08,944 --> 00:08:11,595
so they have to be produced one at a time.

154
00:08:11,595 --> 00:08:15,699
Each step multiplies the weight matrices by just a single vector.

155
00:08:15,929 --> 00:08:19,897
To do that, the GPU has to stream every one of those 16 gigabytes

156
00:08:19,897 --> 00:08:22,705
of weights out of H B M and through the S M's.

157
00:08:22,705 --> 00:08:24,964
And for each weight, a pair of bytes,

158
00:08:24,964 --> 00:08:27,528
it does just two operations: one multiply,

159
00:08:27,528 --> 00:08:30,763
one add. That's an arithmetic intensity of about one.

160
00:08:30,763 --> 00:08:33,327
Way, way down at the left of the roofline.

161
00:08:33,563 --> 00:08:38,802
Let's put numbers on it. Reading 16 gigabytes at 3.35 terabytes per second takes

162
00:08:38,802 --> 00:08:41,486
about five milliseconds. The math itself,

163
00:08:41,486 --> 00:08:47,053
around 16 billion operations, would take the tensor cores about sixteen microseconds.

164
00:08:47,053 --> 00:08:52,095
So during decode, the compute units are idle more than ninety nine percent of

165
00:08:52,095 --> 00:08:52,684
the time.

166
00:08:52,929 --> 00:08:54,645
This sets a hard speed limit.

167
00:08:54,645 --> 00:08:56,419
No matter how clever the code,

168
00:08:56,419 --> 00:09:00,619
one user on this setup can never get more than about two hundred tokens

169
00:09:00,619 --> 00:09:05,233
per second. The speed of text generation is fundamentally the speed of memory.

170
00:09:08,460 --> 00:09:11,748
So how do companies serve millions of people with such hardware?

171
00:09:11,748 --> 00:09:13,084
The key trick is batching.

172
00:09:13,326 --> 00:09:17,604
If a hundred different users are each waiting for their next token,

173
00:09:17,604 --> 00:09:22,073
the server can stack their hundred vectors side by side into a matrix.

174
00:09:22,073 --> 00:09:24,818
Now each weight is loaded from memory once,

175
00:09:24,818 --> 00:09:26,414
but used a hundred times.

176
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:30,038
Since the time per step was dominated by reading the weights,

177
00:09:30,038 --> 00:09:33,637
processing a hundred users costs barely more than processing one.

178
00:09:33,637 --> 00:09:36,351
Throughput grows almost linearly with batch size,

179
00:09:36,351 --> 00:09:39,119
and the point on the roofline slides to the right,

180
00:09:39,119 --> 00:09:40,061
toward the ridge.

181
00:09:40,293 --> 00:09:44,294
In practice, requests arrive and finish at different times,

182
00:09:44,294 --> 00:09:47,143
so modern servers use continuous batching:

183
00:09:47,143 --> 00:09:49,855
at every step, finished sequences leave,

184
00:09:49,855 --> 00:09:52,975
and waiting requests jump into the open slots,

185
00:09:52,975 --> 00:09:54,399
keeping the GPU full.

186
00:09:54,626 --> 00:09:57,147
But batching runs into a second memory problem,

187
00:09:57,147 --> 00:09:59,720
and to see it, we need to look inside attention.

188
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:06,310
Remember that in attention, each token produces a query,

189
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:10,497
a key, and a value. The new token's query is compared against the keys

190
00:10:10,497 --> 00:10:15,342
of every previous token, and those comparisons decide how to weight their values.

191
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:19,999
During decode, the keys and values of earlier tokens never change.

192
00:10:19,999 --> 00:10:22,891
So rather than recomputing them every step,

193
00:10:22,891 --> 00:10:25,312
we compute them once and store them.

194
00:10:25,312 --> 00:10:27,263
This is called the K V cache.

195
00:10:27,493 --> 00:10:30,570
How big is it? For our 8 billion parameter model,

196
00:10:30,570 --> 00:10:32,830
each token stores a key and a value,

197
00:10:32,830 --> 00:10:36,661
in each of 32 layers, for 8 key-value heads of dimension 128,

198
00:10:36,661 --> 00:10:40,868
at two bytes each. That comes out to about 128 kilobytes per token.

199
00:10:42,293 --> 00:10:45,536
That doesn't sound like much, until you multiply it out.

200
00:10:45,536 --> 00:10:50,343
A single conversation with eight thousand tokens of context needs about a gigabyte.

201
00:10:50,343 --> 00:10:54,282
A batch of sixty four such conversations needs sixty four gigabytes,

202
00:10:54,282 --> 00:10:57,235
four times as much as the model weights themselves!

203
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:01,668
And at every decode step, that entire cache has to be read from memory

204
00:11:01,668 --> 00:11:04,975
too. So the K V cache limits how big your batch can be,

205
00:11:04,975 --> 00:11:06,899
and it adds more memory traffic:

206
00:11:06,899 --> 00:11:09,424
the exact resource we're already short on.

207
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:13,444
Early serving systems also wasted a lot of this memory,

208
00:11:13,444 --> 00:11:18,811
reserving one big contiguous chunk for each request's maximum possible length.

209
00:11:18,811 --> 00:11:23,833
v L L M's Paged Attention fixed this with an idea borrowed from operating

210
00:11:23,833 --> 00:11:26,310
systems: split the cache into small,

211
00:11:26,310 --> 00:11:29,751
fixed size pages that can live anywhere in memory,

212
00:11:29,751 --> 00:11:33,328
and keep a table of where each sequence's pages are.

213
00:11:33,328 --> 00:11:37,663
Almost nothing is wasted, so many more requests fit in a batch.

214
00:11:40,893 --> 00:11:44,694
There's one more place where attention fights against the memory hierarchy,

215
00:11:44,694 --> 00:11:46,823
especially during prefill on long prompts.

216
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:51,432
Computing attention naively means forming the full grid of scores,

217
00:11:51,432 --> 00:11:55,141
every query against every key. For a prompt of N tokens,

218
00:11:55,141 --> 00:11:56,731
that's an N by N matrix.

219
00:11:56,731 --> 00:12:01,037
The straightforward approach writes this whole grid out to H B M,

220
00:12:01,037 --> 00:12:03,355
reads it back to apply the softmax,

221
00:12:03,355 --> 00:12:07,860
writes it out again, then reads it back once more to multiply by the

222
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:08,324
values.

223
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:10,870
For long contexts this grid is enormous,

224
00:12:10,870 --> 00:12:14,567
and all that back and forth traffic with H B M dominates the run

225
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:14,855
time.

226
00:12:15,093 --> 00:12:19,552
FlashAttention restructures the computation using the same tiling idea from before.

227
00:12:19,552 --> 00:12:21,862
Load a block of queries into shared memory.

228
00:12:21,862 --> 00:12:25,086
Then stream through the keys and values one block at a time,

229
00:12:25,086 --> 00:12:27,074
computing that small patch of scores,

230
00:12:27,074 --> 00:12:29,330
and folding it into the output right away.

231
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:31,505
The tricky part is the softmax,

232
00:12:31,505 --> 00:12:35,647
which normally needs to see an entire row before it can normalize.

233
00:12:35,647 --> 00:12:39,914
The solution: keep a running maximum and a running sum for each row.

234
00:12:39,914 --> 00:12:42,801
Whenever a new block reveals a larger maximum,

235
00:12:42,801 --> 00:12:45,938
you simply rescale what you've accumulated so far.

236
00:12:45,938 --> 00:12:48,260
The final answer is exactly the same,

237
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:51,712
but the giant N by N matrix never touches H B M at all.

238
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:54,642
Same math, a fraction of the memory traffic,

239
00:12:54,642 --> 00:12:59,396
and often several times faster. It's a beautiful example of a recurring theme:

240
00:12:59,396 --> 00:13:04,394
on modern hardware, the cleverest algorithm is often the one that moves the fewest

241
00:13:04,394 --> 00:13:04,760
bytes.

242
00:13:07,988 --> 00:13:10,248
Once you see the world in terms of bytes moved,

243
00:13:10,248 --> 00:13:13,421
a lot of the other tricks in modern inference start to make sense.

244
00:13:13,655 --> 00:13:16,490
Quantization stores each weight with fewer bits:

245
00:13:16,490 --> 00:13:19,090
say eight, or even four, instead of sixteen.

246
00:13:19,090 --> 00:13:22,162
Fewer bytes to read per token means faster decoding,

247
00:13:22,162 --> 00:13:23,816
almost in direct proportion.

248
00:13:24,055 --> 00:13:28,815
Speculative decoding uses a small, fast draft model to guess the next several tokens.

249
00:13:28,815 --> 00:13:32,399
The big model then checks all of those guesses in a single pass.

250
00:13:32,399 --> 00:13:35,815
Since checking several tokens at once is like a tiny prefill,

251
00:13:35,815 --> 00:13:38,167
it costs about the same as generating one.

252
00:13:38,167 --> 00:13:41,975
Every guess that turns out right is a token you got nearly for free.

253
00:13:42,221 --> 00:13:44,367
And for models too large for one chip,

254
00:13:44,367 --> 00:13:46,569
the weights are split across many GPUs,

255
00:13:46,569 --> 00:13:48,772
connected by very high bandwidth links,

256
00:13:48,772 --> 00:13:51,651
so that each chip only has to stream its own slice.

257
00:13:51,888 --> 00:13:55,542
So next time you watch an answer stream onto your screen,

258
00:13:55,542 --> 00:13:58,298
picture what's underneath. For every token,

259
00:13:58,298 --> 00:14:01,952
billions of weights flow up out of high bandwidth memory,

260
00:14:01,952 --> 00:14:06,374
into thousands of tiny arithmetic units that finish almost instantly,

261
00:14:06,374 --> 00:14:07,849
and then wait for more.

262
00:14:08,155 --> 00:14:10,413
The math was never really the hard part.

263
00:14:10,413 --> 00:14:11,995
The hard part is feeding it.
